I have got an all-SCSI 200i2, with no IDE drives, and I can sucessfully boot
every bootable CD I've been given. As like as not, it may be an issue with the
CD drive you have in the box, which may not be able to recognise a boot
structure on the disk properly. I swapped the original IDE CD ROM drive for an
UltraPlex 32X which boots everything OK.

All the BIOS options do is ensure that a suitable BIOS INT 13 intercept is ready
for the drive. This seems to work OK: I've also had to set up boot support for
the drive number of the Ultraplex in the Adaptec card, but that's been it.

I have got a bootable 266i (single Pentium 2) which does have a better, later
IDE CD ROM drive, and this also has no problems.

