them.  I added one of these, blank drives, to a ds10 (laurel), built the 
bootable drive from cd. Booted it, works fine, configured the beast, and 
put it in operation.

I got my next DS10 (hardy), I popped it open, found the IDE CDRom was 
not cabled (not an issue), saw the lil RZ drive, a floppy, SCSI 3 
controller, the standard one in a DS10, and booted the machine. Works 
great, but has freebsd on it.  I want NetBSD as my choice on the alpha 
boses, just a little idiosyncratic behavior.  SO, I shut down the 
system, pop it open, put bootable drive that was in the OTHER DS10 
(laurel) (the one that was and is running fine) cabled it up right, 
removing the RZ, pluggin in the IBM, both are scsi3, different 
addresses, powered up, did the diags, did the show deve, I see one 
drive, the new IBM from Laurel, and I boot.  The boot proceeds as nomral 
but after a couple of minutes, it hangs.  So, followed the same process, 
and built another bootable drive ffrom CD back on Laurel.  Hardy has the 
known good drive that was in Laurel, but does not boot all the way, and 
laurel is now building a new bootable drive for me.  Completed 
successfully, booted, ran the machine for a couple of days, shut it 
