In article <ufKcnSXzm8eLgQujXTWcqA@speakeasy.net>, "Timothy Stark"
<sword7nospam@speakeasy.org> wrote:

>About SRM console, I noticed that SRM console provides two console methods -
>graphics or serial.  If SRM console finds no graphics device, then attempts
>to use serial ports for console? Right?  Does it require graphics device to
>display console information?

This behaviour varies a lot, depending on the alpha platform.

Some systems sense a switch position when they power up, and use graphics
or serial console accordingly.

Some systems check the value of the CONSOLE environment variable.

Some systems will switch to the "other" console port automatically if they
find missing hardware.  For example, if the the keyboard is missing, they
switch to serial console.

Most of the newer systems will automatically communicate on any of the
console ports when you start typing there.  All the console commands work,
but shortly after the OS boot begins, the output switches to the port set
by the CONSOLE environment variable, and the other port(s) go silent.


