JF Mezei <jfmezei.spamnot@vl.videotron.ca> wrote in message news:<3E908114.BA58E316@vl.videotron.ca>...
> The only problem with this is what happens when the VMS
> engineers are forced to make Alpha-VMS mature and stop developing it ?

That's where the common-source-code decision really begins to shine.

During the VAX-to-Alpha transition, the Alpha code base was separated
from the VAX code base.  Initially this allowed VAX features to run
ahead of Alpha unfettered (e.g. V6.0), but also meant that extra
effort was needed in the early years for Alpha features to catch up
with VAX, and years later, now it means it takes an extra step (and
thus extra effort) for any new features developed for Alpha to also be
included for VAX.

With the common source-code base that exists between Alpha and
Itanium, any new features developed on Alpha in the next few years
will be included on Itanium by default, and in the more-distant
future, new features developed on Itanium will be included on Alpha by
default, unless extra effort is taken to specifically exclude them.


