You cannot use an HSZ50 as a transparent upgrade/replacement for an HSZ40 
and vice versa.  This is stated clearly in all HSZ50 firmware release 
notes.  An HSZ40 cannot load meta configuration for an HSZ50 from a drive 
with saved meta config info and vice versa.  The configurations are not 
compatible.  You have to dump and fatfinger the configurations.  You'd 
better have a screen dump of all controller config info before you even 
think of attempting something like this.

Setting any HSZXX to initial configuration will do exactly that - zap the 
configuration and all licenses.  That's the intent of the CLI set 
this_controller initial...

If you know the configurations and names of the arrays, you can certainly 
use the HSZ40 or 50 as swap replacements, but - again, you will have to 
remove any existing configurations from the controller and fatfinger the 
correct array info back in.  If you are not familiar with the CLI for HSZ 
controllers, use CFMENU to remove the old configurations and add the new 
ones.  If you don't have a well documented configuration you're in 
trouble.

Do not initialize the drives, simply match the configurations that existed 
on the previous controller exactly and they will work perfectly, as long 
as you have the appropriate licenses.  If you initialize the drives, the 
information on them is immediately dust.

Another note.  Depite the fact that there is existing meta config space on 
a drive initialized by either HSZ controller, neither controller will use 
that space after a swap.  Use the CLI, set one drive with reserved meta 
space to write protect status, restart the controller and then unwrite 
protect the drive.  It will then write the controller config to the drive 
with reserved meta space.  Do it before you try and boot something.

Unless you have a firmware license card or a way to fat finger the license 
codes in you're out of luck.  

However, if you have a second HSZ40, you can install both controllers and 
set failover copy from the good controller to the one you zapped and it 
will add the licenses it has to the zapped controller.  Ensure you do this 
correctly or you will zero the good controller.  No you cannot use an 
HSZ50 to do this.

Most HSZXX controllers had some form of basic striping and mirroring 
licensing out of the box.  Otherwise they were just high priced hardware 
with no more capabilities than your average SCSI controller carrying a ton 
of cache.



